`iterrows` runs a Python-level loop and constructs a new `Series` for every row (with dtype upcasting — ints may become floats when mixed with floats). Per-row overhead is microseconds, so a few million rows means minutes.

Instead, in order of preference:
- column arithmetic and `.str`/`.dt` accessors;
- `np.where` / `np.select` for conditionals;
- `Series.map(dict)` or `merge` for lookups;
- `groupby().agg()` / `transform()` for per-group logic;
- if a loop is truly required, `itertuples(index=False)` (namedtuples, much faster) or a list comprehension over `zip(df.a, df.b)`.

```python
df["net"] = np.where(df.type.eq("refund"), -df.amount, df.amount)
```
